---
import 'modern-normalize/modern-normalize.css'

export const prerender: boolean = true
---

<html lang="en">
  <head>
    <meta charset="utf-8">
    <link rel="icon" type="image/svg+xml" href="/favicon.svg">
    <link rel="icon" href="/favicon.ico">
    <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width">
    <meta name="generator" content={Astro.generator}>
    <title>avif.now - Frequently Asked Questions</title>
  </head>
  <body>
    <header>
      <div class="restrict-width header-container">
        <h1><a href="/">avif.now</a> FAQ</h1>
      </div>
    </header>

    <main>
      <div class="restrict-width">
        <article>
          <h2>Are my files ever uploaded?</h2>
          <p>
            The conversion from video to AVIF is slow mainly because it happens
            in your browser. The file you select for AVIF conversion is never
            uploaded to a backend server.
          </p>
          <p>
            Tenor URLs on the other hand <em>are</em> sent to the backend
            server, as retrieving the URL of the MP4 from the Tenor View URL
            can't be done in a browser.
          </p>
        </article>

        <article>
          <h2>Why is there a Tenor option?</h2>
          <p>
            With Tenor sunsetting its API, and being owned by Google, I wouldn't
            be surprised if Tenor eventually just stops working in Discord or is
            just shut down altogether. I don't think this will happen for a long
            long time (if it even happens at all), but I want to get a head
            start.
          </p>
        </article>

        <article>
          <h2>Why not WebP?</h2>
          <p>
            For short video clips and the stuff you'd find on Tenor, there's
            really not much of a difference. I'm simply just a fan of AV1. While
            this webpage encodes 8-bit color to make hardware decoding more
            likely, AVIF does happen to support 10-bit color. Additionally, for
            lossy compression, AVIF beats out WebP on filesize (at the cost of
            encoding speed).
          </p>
        </article>

        <article>
          <h2>What flags does the website use?</h2>
          <p>
            The website uses <code>libaom-av1</code> instead of{' '}
            <code>libsvtav1</code>, with <code>-cpu-used 4</code>.{' '}
            <code>libsvtav1</code>{' '}
            doesn't run well in browsers, and a{' '}
            <code>-cpu-used</code>{' '}
            lower (higher-effort) than 4 would result in untenable encoding
            times.
          </p>
        </article>

        <article>
          <h2 id="encoding-flags">How can I squeeze out extra quality?</h2>
          <p>
            The two easiest ways to get extra quality out of the encoder are:
          </p>
          <ol>
            <li>
              lower <code>-preset</code> towards 0 (longer encoding time for
              better quality)
            </li>
            <li>
              lower <code>-crf</code> towards 22 (higher filesize for better
              quality, 22 is roughly the floor for perceptual losslessness)
            </li>
          </ol>
          <p>
            However, if you know things about your input file, you can do better
            by tuning the AV1 encoder. <code>libsvtav1</code> has flags (and{' '}
            <code>ffmpeg</code>{' '}
            has filters) that allow you to do this. The default{' '}
            <code>-svtav1-params</code>{' '}
            provided by avif.now are:
          </p>
          <ol>
            <li><code>tune=0</code></li>
            <li><code>enable-variance-boost=1</code></li>
            <li><code>enable-qm=1</code> and <code>qm-min=4</code></li>
          </ol>

          <h3>Gradients</h3>
          <p>
            If your source file has many gradients, and the default settings
            result in noticable color banding,
          </p>
          <ol>
            <li>
              add <code>-pix_fmt yuv420p10le</code> after{' '}
              <code>-c:v libsvtav1</code>.
            </li>
          </ol>

          <h3>Blocky Video</h3>
          <p>
            If your source file is low-quality from a social media website (for
            example Twitter),
          </p>
          <ol>
            <li>
              add <code>-vf "deblock=filter=strong:block=4"</code> before{' '}
              <code>-c:v libsvtav1</code>
            </li>
            <li>
              use <code>tune=1</code> instead of <code>tune=0</code> in{' '}
              <code>-svtav1-params</code>
            </li>
            <li>
              add <code>enable-dlf=2</code>, <code>enable-cdef=1</code>, and{' '}
              <code>enable-restoration=1</code>{' '}
              to <code>-svtav1-params</code>
            </li>
          </ol>

          <h3>Noisy Video</h3>
          <p>If your source file makes heavy use of noise or static,</p>
          <ol>
            <li>
              add <code>film-grain=25</code> and{' '}
              <code>film-grain-denoise=1</code>{' '}
              to <code>-svtav1-params</code>
            </li>
            <li>
              use <code>qm-min=0</code> instead of <code>qm-min=4</code> in{' '}
              <code>-svtav1-params</code>
            </li>
          </ol>

          <h3>Anime Clips</h3>
          <p>If your source file is 2d animation / animated clips,</p>
          <ol>
            <li>
              add <code>enable-variance-boost=1</code> to{' '}
              <code>-svtav1-params</code>
            </li>
            <li>add <code>qm-max=8</code> to <code>-svtav1-params</code></li>
            <li>
              add <code>tf-strength=1</code> to <code>-svtav1-params</code>
            </li>
            <li>add <code>scm=2</code> to <code>-svtav1-params</code></li>
          </ol>

          <h3>High-Detail Soft-Edge 3D (most 3D games)</h3>
          <p>
            If your source file is footage of a 3d game with soft world edges
            (and optionally hard UI edges),
          </p>
          <ol>
            <li>add <code>sharpness=1</code> to <code>-svtav1-params</code></li>
            <li>
              add <code>variance-boost-strength=2</code> to{' '}
              <code>-svtav1-params</code>
            </li>
            <li>
              add <code>tf-strength=1</code> to <code>-svtav1-params</code>
            </li>
          </ol>

          <h3>High-Detail Hard-Edge 3D (e.g. Minecraft with Shaders)</h3>
          <p>
            If your source file is footage of a 3d game with hard world edges,
          </p>
          <ol>
            <li>add <code>scm=0</code> to <code>-svtav1-params</code></li>
            <li>
              add <code>variance-boost-strength=3</code> to{' '}
              <code>-svtav1-params</code>
            </li>
            <li>
              add <code>film-grain=4</code>, <code>film-grain-denoise=0</code>,
              and <code>tf-strength=1</code> to <code>-svtav1-params</code>
            </li>
          </ol>

          <h3>Low-Detail Hard-Edge 3D (e.g. Vanilla Minecraft)</h3>
          <p>
            If your source file is footage of a 3d game with hard world edges
            and mostly flat colors,
          </p>
          <ol>
            <li>add <code>scm=1</code> to <code>-svtav1-params</code></li>
            <li>
              add <code>sharpness=2</code> and{' '}
              <code>enable-restoration=0</code>{' '}
              to <code>-svtav1-params</code>
            </li>
          </ol>
        </article>
      </div>
    </main>

    <footer>
      <div class="slight-restrict-width footer-container">
        <p>
          Made with love and{' '}
          <a href="https://github.com/Yahweasel/libav.js">libav.js</a>{' '}
          &#x2764;&#xfe0e;
        </p>
        <p>
          Not affiliated with Alliance for Open Media.{' '}
          <a href="https://github.com/s5bug/avif.now">Source</a>
        </p>
      </div>
    </footer>
  </body>
</html>

<style>
:root {
  color-scheme: dark light;

  --bg-header-footer: #cccccc;
  --bg-main: #ffffff;
  --text-color: #000000;
}

@media (prefers-color-scheme: dark) {
  :root {
    --bg-header-footer: #000000;
    --bg-main: #222222;
    --text-color: #ffffff;
  }
}

html {
  background-color: var(--bg-header-footer);
}

header,
main,
section,
footer {
  padding: 8px;
}

main {
  background-color: var(--bg-main);
}

header,
footer,
section {
  background-color: var(--bg-header-footer);
}

.restrict-width {
  max-width: 800px;
  margin: 0 auto;
}

.slight-restrict-width {
  max-width: 1200px;
  margin: 0 auto;
}

.header-container {
  display: grid;
  grid-template-columns: 1fr 1fr 1fr;
}

.header-container > h1 {
  grid-column: 2;
  text-align: center;
}

.header-container > nav {
  grid-column: 3;
  text-align: right;
}

.footer-container {
  display: flex;
  justify-content: space-between;
}
</style>
